      "id": "post-1780998542956",
      "seriesId": "nis2-landing-zone",
      "part": 0,
      "title": "Building a NIS2-Compliant AWS Landing Zone — Week 0: Why I'm Doing This",
      "date": "2026-06-09",
      "excerpt": "On 17 October 2024 the EU's NIS2 Directive came into force — yet eighteen months later, almost no public Terraform examples mention it by name. This series fills that gap: an AWS landing zone mapped to NIS2 and ISO 27001, built in public over six weeks.",
      "content": "NIS2 came into force across the EU in October 2024. Thousands of companies in banking, healthcare, energy, and telecom now have a legal obligation to implement the cybersecurity measures in Article 21 — encryption, incident handling, access control, continuous risk management. The deadline has passed. The fines reach €10M or 2% of global turnover.\n\nAnd yet, search \"AWS landing zone Terraform\" today and almost nothing mentions NIS2 by name. Plenty of tutorials map to CIS or NIST. The regulation that actually creates legal obligations for EU companies is missing from public infrastructure-as-code.\n\nThat's the gap I'm filling. Over six weeks, I'm building an open-source AWS landing zone in Terraform, where every module maps to a specific NIS2 Article 21 measure and ISO 27001:2022 control — built in public, module by module, with the messy parts visible.\n\n<img src='/blog/week 0.gif' alt='the plan' style='width:100%;border-radius:12px' />\n\n> *The whole plan in one frame: stage tracker (Scope → Build → Validate), the six week-panels with their modules and NIS2 measures, and the coverage chain showing how the regulation gets satisfied week by week. This replaces a long text walkthrough — the reader sees the arc, then the prose below explains the why.*\n\n\n## The approach, in three decisions\n\n**Two frameworks, mapped deeply.** NIS2 is the *what* (the legal requirement); ISO 27001:2022 is the *how* (the auditable controls EU teams use to demonstrate it). I deliberately skipped CIS and NIST — two frameworks mapped deeply shows more than five mapped shallowly. Every resource is tagged with the measure and control it satisfies, so the compliance mapping is generated from the infrastructure itself, not a spreadsheet that drifts.\n\n**LocalStack for the build, real AWS for the proof.** Everything is developed and tested on LocalStack Pro at zero cost — and anyone can clone the repo and run it without an AWS account. A single cost-controlled run on real AWS in Week 6 produces the authentic evidence (real GuardDuty findings, Security Hub dashboards) that an emulator can't, for under €15.\n\n**Managed risk, not zero-findings theater.** Most \"secure infra\" demos chase a clean scanner dashboard with everything suppressed. That's theater. A real posture has open findings — each one seen, judged, and recorded with a reason. The decision matrix that records those calls is the actual compliance artifact. The green badge is just the honest side effect.\n\n## Building in public\n\nThe repository is public from day one — rough commits, WIP branches, bot PRs and all. Every architectural choice gets an Architecture Decision Record. Every scanner finding gets a documented disposition. This blog series is the narrative thread: six posts, one per week, each picking up where the last left off.\n\nI'm writing it as a build journal rather than a set of tutorials because the *journey* is the artifact. A polished final repo hides every decision made along the way — you can't tell what was obvious from the start and what was a hard-won refactor. The series makes that visible.\n\n## Next week in the series\n\nPost 2 covers Week 1: the foundation modules — a KMS key and a secure-by-default S3 bucket, composed through a clean interface — plus native `terraform test` and a full CI pipeline with branch protection. By the end of Week 1 the repo has two modules, nine tests, seven CI checks on every PR, and a green badge that means something.\n\nRepo Link: [github.com/olanak/aws-nis2-baseline](https://github.com/olanak/aws-nis2-baseline)\n\n---",
